Chapter: ScopeConsole

 The allow half of the scope check for the console family. Everything
 here sits inside the scope its signature grants, so it compiles and
 runs. The refusal half -- for the console channel and for the network
 authority both -- is codex/test/errors/scope-network-port.

 A console grant is a CHANNEL, not a path prefix. The channel is
 decided by which function was called and never by an argument the
 caller computed, so this is the one scoped check that cannot be
 defeated by an unprovable argument: print-error-uni is stderr no
 matter what it is handed.

 A signature that grants Console.Write "stdout" therefore admits
 print-line-uni and refuses print-error-uni, and the parent row
 Console admits both -- which is what every program in the tree that
 declares a bare [Console] relies on.

 THERE IS NO NETWORK HALF HERE, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. The
 network scope relation is real and enforced -- the errors test proves
 it refuses a wrong port and a suffixed host -- but the operations it
 scopes (fetch, post, resolve-dns) are declared in Foreword chapter
 Network with no handler anywhere in the tree, so a program that calls
 one cannot be emitted (CDX2040) and the ALLOW direction cannot be
 exercised end to end by anybody. See docs/PM/BACKLOG.md 1.7.

Section: Console

  say : Text -> [Console.Write "stdout"] Nothing
  say (m) = print-line-uni m

  wide : Text -> [Console] Nothing
  wide (m) = print-line-uni m

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    say "console scope holds"
    wide "a bare Console grant still covers Console.Write"
  end
